//# Imports

import ./types.vibe {
  Type, collect_type_var_ids, type_erase_vars, type_has_unknown
}

// Standard host-provider and entry-execution policy is a sibling in this
// package, so the drift guard below reads it directly instead of through the
// @vibe/compiler/core facade.
import ./standard_effect_policy.vibe {
  has_standard_effect_policy
}

import @vibe/core {
  array_empty
}

import ./sorted_index.vibe {
  bsearch_leftmost, sort_perm_by_names
}

//# Functions

// #415: the central declarative registry of func-table builtins across
// both backends. Row: (name, param_count, returns_value 0|1,
// in_linear_table, in_gc_table).
//
// B-1: this registry DRIVES both lanes' registration. The linear builder
// (wasi/linked_compile.vibe) and the gc builder (gc/backend_body.vibe) each
// loop over these rows (filtered by their lane flag) and only resolve the
// lane-local function/body INDEX via a per-lane match — names, param counts
// and returns exist ONLY here. Adding a builtin = one row here + one index
// match arm in each claiming lane (+ the import/body wiring). A row claiming
// a lane whose index match has no arm fails every build by name; the
// remaining verify_lane_builtins pass (below) guards any future hand-edited
// registration path against drifting from this table (the wasm-gc HOF-gap
// class of regression this issue exists to prevent).
//
// #2584: a new row also has to be CLASSIFIED, or explicitly recorded as
// belonging on no list. The four flags on each row (pure / non-allocating /
// borrow-returning / safe-mut) are the audit; the lists the rest of the
// compiler reads are derived from them. Names that are not registry rows
// stay in small extras tables next to the derived lists. A registry name
// with every flag false that is on neither census table is unreviewed --
// tests/builtin_classification_census_test.vibe fails on it, naming every
// list and what a miss on each does.
//
// Lane semantics:
// - in_linear_table / in_gc_table refer to the lane's FUNC-TABLE registration
//   only. A name absent from a lane's table may still be served there by a
//   callsite lowering (expr/compile_call.vibe, gc/backend_call.vibe) or by
//   prelude vibe source — B-1 will classify those; B-0 pins what exists.
// - Host imports (Fs::/Env::/sleep/...) are linear-only by design: the gc
//   lane is the pure-test lane and gates host builtins out as a group.
// B-2: rows now carry the CHECKER TYPE as the single source of truth.
// param_count / returns for the lanes are DERIVED from that type
// (builtin_registry() below); the checker resolves these names through
// lookup_registry_builtin FIRST, so the per-namespace checker lookup files
// no longer need (and must not re-add) arms for registry names.
// checker_visible=false rows are codegen-internal (desugar/compile_call
// artifacts, RC runtime hooks, canonical-name aliases) -- the checker never
// sees those names, and their synthesized types only feed the derived
// param/returns view.

// #1520 提案2: rows spell the unconstrained type by MEANING, not by the raw
// constructor, so a reader can tell a deliberately-unchecked position from a
// placeholder someone forgot to fill in:
//
//   - reg_any()     — deliberately UNRELATED: any value type is legal at this
//                     position and nothing ties it to any other position, so
//                     the checker's head-kind compare tolerates it (`eq` /
//                     `assert_eq`, `__to_string`).
//   - reg_var(n)    — a real type VARIABLE scoped to the row: two positions
//                     written `reg_var(0)` are the SAME type (`Array::get` is
//                     `(Array[T], Int) -> T`). This is the one a first-class
//                     value form can be typed from; see `reg_var` below.
//   - reg_opaque(k) — an internal HANDLE with no checker-type representation
//                     (builder objects, Map). `k` names the handle kind for the
//                     reader; the value is ignored.
//   - bare CtUnknown — not allowed in rows. It reads as "not written yet",
//                     which is exactly how the #1519 `eq`-declared-as-CtInt
//                     class of lie stayed invisible: nothing distinguished a
//                     row nobody had thought about from a row deliberately
//                     left open.
//
// `reg_any()` and `reg_opaque(k)` produce the same runtime value (CtUnknown) —
// that distinction is documentation the type system cannot express, enforced
// by review + the golden positive corpus (builtin_registry_golden_test.vibe),
// not by the checker. `reg_var(n)` is different in kind: it is a real `CtVar`,
// and the checker reads it.
fn reg_any() -> Type {
  CtUnknown
}

/// #2451: an open position that is a real type VARIABLE, scoped to its row.
/// Two positions written `reg_var(0)` are the SAME type; `reg_any()` twice is
/// two independent unknowns.
///
/// This is what a first-class value form needs and `reg_any()` cannot give it.
/// Measured before this existed: `let get = Array::get` then
/// `let s: String = get([1], 0)` checked CLEAN and printed garbage, while the
/// direct `Array::get([1], 0)` in the same position was correctly rejected
/// with `expected String, got Int`. The relationship was simply not in the
/// row, so the value form had nothing to lose it from.
///
/// Ids are row-local and start at 0. `registry_builtin_scheme` closes a row
/// that mentions them into a `CtForAll`, and `instantiate` freshens the ids
/// per use site -- including past the importing module's own counter (#743),
/// which is why a row may use 0 and 1 without colliding with inference.
///
/// Every OTHER consumer of a registry row reads the erased view
/// (`builtin_registry_typed`, where `type_erase_vars` turns each variable back
/// into `CtUnknown`), so adding variables to a row cannot change what the
/// direct-call fast path, the effect walk, the contract checker or the codegen
/// lookups see. That separation is deliberate: unifying a raw `CtVar(0)` from
/// a row against a module's own inference would bind ITS variable 0.
fn reg_var(_ id: Int) -> Type {
  CtVar(id)
}

fn reg_opaque(_ kind: String) -> Type {
  let _ = kind
  CtUnknown
}

fn reg_stdin_stream() -> Type {
  CtStruct("#vibe_stdin_stream")
}

fn reg_p0() -> Array[Type] {
  array_empty()
}

fn reg_p1(_ a: Type) -> Array[Type] {
  let arr = reg_p0()
  Array::push(arr, value = a)
  arr
}

fn reg_p2(a: Type, b: Type) -> Array[Type] {
  let arr = reg_p1(a)
  Array::push(arr, value = b)
  arr
}

fn reg_p3(a: Type, b: Type, c: Type) -> Array[Type] {
  let arr = reg_p2(a = a, b = b)
  Array::push(arr, value = c)
  arr
}

fn reg_p4(a: Type, b: Type, c: Type, d: Type) -> Array[Type] {
  let arr = reg_p3(a = a, b = b, c = c)
  Array::push(arr, value = d)
  arr
}

fn reg_p5(a: Type, b: Type, c: Type, d: Type, e: Type) -> Array[Type] {
  let arr = reg_p4(a = a, b = b, c = c, d = d)
  Array::push(arr, value = e)
  arr
}

/// #2503: the rows as text, one per line -- `NAME TYPE FLAGS`, `#` lines are
/// comments -- decoded once into `registry_typed_rows`. As a literal they were
/// one 66 KB function body of constructor calls the compiler had to compile
/// and run; as text they are a data segment. TYPE is a prefix code:
/// `i` Int, `s` String, `u` Unit, `b` Bool, `y` Bytes, `d` Double, `f` Float,
/// `c` Char, `I` Int32, `U` UInt32, `?` unknown, `oKIND;` an opaque runtime
/// value (unknown to the checker; KIND names it), `S` a stdin stream, `vN;` row
/// variable N, `A`/`O` + type for Array/Option, `NNAME;K;` + K types for a named
/// type, and `FK;` + K parameter types + result + row (`-` none, `!NAME;`) for
/// a function. FLAGS are the seven Bool columns below, `T`/`F` in order.
let registry_rows_text: String = #|Env::get F1;ss!Env; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Env::args_len F0;i!Env; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Env::args_get F1;is!Env; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::read_file F1;ss!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::exists F1;sb!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::stat_token F1;si!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# Checker-visible callsite lowering: raw directory import + String::split.
                                 #|# Neither backend registers a separate func-table entry for this surface.
                                 #|Fs::readdir F1;sAs!Fs; FFTFFFF
                                 #|# #795: alias-collision-proof surface for the same import.
                                 #|vibe_fs_stat_token_raw F1;si!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::write_file F2;ssu!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# Registered but deliberately unused by compiler sources until the cache
                                 #|# publisher adopts it, preserving seed compatibility.
                                 #|Fs::publish_immutable_text F2;ssb!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::write_bytes F2;syu!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::read_bytes F1;sy!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_fs_read_dir_raw F1;ss!Fs; TFFFFFF
                                 #|sh F1;ss!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_sh_lines_raw F1;ss!Process; TFFFFFF
                                 #|# #865: structured subprocess result. `vibe_sh_capture_raw` runs the
                                 #|# command ONCE (unlike a naive 3-accessor design that would re-run it)
                                 #|# and parks {exit_code, stdout, stderr} behind a handle -- same
                                 #|# handle+accessor shape as the #794 HTTP client imports below, chosen so
                                 #|# a structured result doesn't require struct-returning host ABI work.
                                 #|# checker_visible=true (unlike vibe_sh_lines_raw's raw half) because these
                                 #|# are plain qualified names (no bare "sh"/"sh_lines" collision risk), so
                                 #|# ordinary vibe source (lib/@vibe/process/process.vibe) calls them
                                 #|# directly, mirroring lib/@vibe/http/http.vibe's `vibe_http_*_raw`
                                 #|# direct-call convention -- no compile_call.vibe special-casing needed.
                                 #|vibe_sh_capture_raw F1;si!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_sh_capture_exit_code_raw F1;ii!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_sh_capture_stdout_raw F1;is!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_sh_capture_stderr_raw F1;is!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_sh_capture_close_raw F1;iu!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# #903/#865: propagate a guest-chosen exit code to the real OS process
                                 #|# exit status (unlike a `main() -> Int` return, which `_start` only
                                 #|# PRINTS -- see ADR-0069's entry_returns_unit comment in
                                 #|# codegen/wasi/linked_compile.vibe). Traps via the host runner's
                                 #|# existing exit-trap mechanism (Rust `viberun`: reuses ExitTrap, the
                                 #|# same trap the legacy `__moonbit_sys_unstable::exit` import already
                                 #|# used; JS runner: `process.exit(code)` directly) -- never returns.
                                 #|vibe_process_exit_raw F1;iu!Process; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Stdin::read_stream F1;is!Stdin; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Stdout::write_stream F1;su!Stdout; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Stdout::write_char F1;iu!Stdout; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# #865: Stderr, mirroring Stdout::write_stream / Stdout::write_char exactly
                                 #|# (own host effect name "Stderr" so a function using only stderr does not
                                 #|# need to declare `with Stdout`).
                                 #|Stderr::write_stream F1;su!Stderr; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Stderr::write_char F1;iu!Stderr; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# #1460 Phase 1: the `Console` capability -- one effect covering the three
                                 #|# tty streams, replacing Stdin/Stdout/Stderr. Both name sets are live here
                                 #|# ON PURPOSE and only for the length of the migration:
                                 #|#
                                 #|#   the committed seed knows ONLY the legacy three, and the seed is what
                                 #|#   compiles this file. So the compiler must learn `Console` in a build the
                                 #|#   seed can still produce, that build becomes the next seed, and only THEN
                                 #|#   can lib/ (this file included) move to `Console::*`. Deleting the legacy
                                 #|#   entries in the same commit that adds these would make the compiler
                                 #|#   unbuildable from its own seed.
                                 #|#
                                 #|# The stderr pair cannot keep its operation spelling once the effects
                                 #|# merge -- `Console::write_stream` is already taken by stdout -- so the
                                 #|# error side is named apart rather than disambiguated by effect.
                                 #|#
                                 #|# Phase 3 removes the six legacy entries above and this comment with them.
                                 #|Console::read_stream F1;is!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Console::read_char F0;i!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Console::write_stream F1;su!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Console::write_char F1;iu!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Console::write_err_stream F1;su!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Console::write_err_char F1;iu!Console; TFTFFFF
                                 #|resolve_path F1;ss- TFFFFFF
                                 #|Fs::remove F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::remove_file F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::remove_tree F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::mkdir_p F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::mkdir F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::chdir F1;su!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::copy F2;ssu!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::append F2;ssu!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::rename F2;ssu!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::is_dir F1;sb!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::is_file F1;sb!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|Fs::getcwd F0;s!Fs; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# Socket client host imports (vibe.tcp_*) -- same "checker-legal (see
                                 #|# builtins/declarations.vibe), zero codegen wiring" gap the Fs::* rows
                                 #|# above closed. #1232 review: raw dunder names, NOT `Socket::tcp_connect`
                                 #|# -- lib/@vibe/socket's package index.vpkg ALSO declares `effect
                                 #|# TcpSocket { Connect(String,Int)->Int; Read(Int,Int)->String;
                                 #|# Write(Int,String)->Unit; Close(Int)->Unit }` for its in-package
                                 #|# mock-handler tests, and that effect's operations are STRUCTURALLY
                                 #|# IDENTICAL in signature to these builtins -- a qualified call under the
                                 #|# `Socket::` name let the checker's effect-row resolution match BOTH the
                                 #|# builtin's own row and TcpSocket's, unioning a spurious `{ TcpSocket }`
                                 #|# requirement onto every caller. Same collision class Http's client-side
                                 #|# functions (lib/@vibe/http/http.vibe) already document and avoid via
                                 #|# `vibe_http_*_raw` naming -- follow that precedent here too.
                                 #|# checker_visible=true: no bare-name collision risk, so ordinary vibe
                                 #|# source (lib/@vibe/socket/tcp.vibe's low-level layer) calls them
                                 #|# directly, same convention as Fs::*/Http's client raw calls -- no
                                 #|# compile_call.vibe special-casing needed. Supersedes the now-dead
                                 #|# Socket::tcp_* arms in checker/builtin_sigs/builtins_net.vibe (lookup_registry_builtin
                                 #|# runs first; this file's file-header comment: per-namespace checker
                                 #|# lookup files "must not re-add" arms for registry names).
                                 #|vibe_tcp_connect_raw F2;sii!Socket; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_tcp_read_raw F2;iis!Socket; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_tcp_write_raw F2;isu!Socket; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_tcp_close_raw F1;iu!Socket; TFTFFFF
                                 #|# #794: HTTP client host imports (vibe.http_*) -- host builtins are
                                 #|# linear-only by design (the gc lane is the pure-test lane).
                                 #|vibe_http_request_raw F4;ssssi!Http; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_http_response_status_raw F1;ii!Http; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_http_response_header_raw F2;iss!Http; TFTFFFF
                                 #|vibe_http_response_body_raw F1;is!Http; TFTFFFF
